B.DO=new A.ce(1,"Take the win",B.fl,"Four of your pieces in a line \u2014 across, up, or diagonally \u2014 ends the game. Before anything clever, check whether you already have three with an open end.",B.fi,B.F9,3,B.an,"Three amber sunburst pieces sit on the bottom row in columns 1, 2 and 3. Column 4 completes the row. It also happens to stop the teal diamonds doing the same thing from the other side \u2014 but you would play it anyway, because winning ends the game before any threat of theirs matters.")
B.fj=new A.h6(2,"blockNow")
B.Fb=s([0,1,0,2,0],t.t)
B.DU=new A.ce(2,"Stop the stack",B.fl,"A column filling up with one colour is the easiest threat to see and the easiest to stop. Three stacked pieces need only one more.",B.fj,B.Fb,0,B.an,"Amber sunburst has three in column 1. Dropping into that column takes the square they need. Every other move on the board loses immediately, however good it looks.")
B.Fh=s([0,1,6,2,6,3],t.t)
B.DW=new A.ce(3,"Stop the row",B.fl,"A horizontal three has two ends. If one end is already blocked, only one square matters \u2014 find it before you look anywhere else.",B.fj,B.Fh,4,B.an,"The teal diamonds hold columns 2, 3 and 4 on the bottom row. The left end, column 1, is already yours. That leaves column 5 as the only square that finishes their row, so that is the only move.")
B.DD=new A.h6(8,"mostLines")
B.Em=s([3,0],t.t)
B.DN=new A.ce(4,"The middle is worth more",B.iW,"A piece in the centre belongs to more possible fours than a piece on the edge \u2014 more rows, more diagonals. Nothing in the rules says so; it falls out of the shape of the board. Count them and see.",B.DD,B.Em,3,B.an,"Thirteen different fours pass through the square you just took, more than through any other square available this turn. An edge square sits on far fewer. That single fact is why strong players open in the middle and fight for it afterwards.")
B.F4=s([2,0,4,3,3,4,4,0,5,5,3],t.t)
B.EJ=s([5],t.t)
B.DG=new A.ce(5,"The square you must not fill",B.iW,"You never choose a square, only a column \u2014 and your piece makes the square above it available to them. Sometimes one column is poisoned and every other move is fine.",B.iV,B.F4,null,B.EJ,"Dropping into column 6 puts your piece directly under the square the amber sunbursts need, and they take it on the next turn. Nothing else on the board is urgent, so simply play elsewhere. Before any move, ask what your piece opens up, not only what it builds.")
B.fk=new A.h6(3,"forkNow")
B.Ek=s([2,0,4,0],t.t)
B.DK=new A.ce(6,"Two ways to win",B.bO,"One threat gets blocked. Two threats at once cannot both be blocked, because the opponent only gets one move.",B.fk,B.Ek,3,B.an,"You already hold columns 3 and 5 on the bottom row. Filling column 4 between them makes a run of three with both ends open, and both ends are reachable right now. They block one; you take the other.")
B.DC=new A.h6(4,"stopFork")
B.E9=s([0,2,0,4],t.t)
B.DV=new A.ce(7,"Take the square they want",B.bO,"The move that would be strongest for your opponent is often the strongest move for you. Before playing, ask what you would do if it were their turn.",B.DC,B.E9,3,B.an,"If the teal diamonds get column 4, they hold three on the bottom row with both ends open and you cannot cover both. Taking that square first costs you nothing and removes the whole plan.")
B.FS=s([2,2,6,3,6,3,4,4],t.t)
B.Ef=s([1,5],t.t)
B.DT=new A.ce(8,"Do not build their staircase",B.bO,"Your piece fills a square, and the square directly above it becomes available \u2014 to them. A move can be safe in itself and still hand over the game.",B.mA,B.FS,null,B.Ef,"The teal diamonds have three in a row on the second row, needing either end. Neither end is reachable yet. Play column 2 or column 6 and your own piece becomes the step they climb. Any other column is fine.")
B.Gc=s([2,1,3,2,3,3,6,4,4,4],t.t)
B.DJ=new A.ce(9,"Diagonals are the ones you miss",B.bO,"Rows and columns are easy to scan. Diagonals cut across both and are the lines most games are actually lost to. Check them last, every single turn.",B.fj,B.Gc,4,B.an,"The teal diamonds run diagonally up from the bottom of column 2. Column 5 already holds three pieces, so their fourth square is reachable on their next turn. Dropping into column 5 takes it.")
B.FR=s([0,4,1,4,2,4],t.t)
B.DP=new A.ce(10,"Winning beats blocking",B.bO,"If you can finish, finish. A threat you never have to face is not a threat.",B.fi,B.FR,3,B.an,"The teal diamonds have three stacked in column 5 and it is tempting to block. But column 4 completes your bottom row now, and the game ends before they ever play. Check for your own win before you check for theirs.")
B.Gf=s([3,2,0,1,1,2,2,3,3,6],t.t)
B.DL=new A.ce(11,"A diagonal you can finish",B.bO,"Once a column has filled up under a diagonal, the square at the top of it becomes reachable \u2014 often several moves after you set the line up and stopped watching it.",B.fi,B.Gf,3,B.an,"Your pieces climb from the bottom of column 1 to column 3. Column 4 is now three deep, so the next drop lands exactly on the fourth square of that diagonal. Lines mature; keep looking at old ones.")
B.Fq=s([5,2,2,4,3,3,2,0,0,5,1,1,4,0],t.t)
B.DM=new A.ce(12,"Build the double threat",B.bO,"A single threat is an invitation to block. Two threats made by one move end the game, because they only get one turn. Look for the square that finishes two lines at once.",B.fk,B.Fq,2,B.an,"Column 3 leaves the amber sunbursts with two separate squares that each complete a four, and the teal diamonds cannot cover both. Note that neither threat existed before the move: the fork was created, not found.")
B.En=s([3,3,2,4],t.t)
B.DF=new A.ce(13,"Count the empty squares",B.bw,"The board holds 42 squares. If you move first you take squares 1, 3, 5 and so on, so on a full board you take the odd ones. Whose turn it is when a key square opens is not luck; it is arithmetic.",B.dP,B.En,null,B.an,"When you find a square that decides the game, count how many empty squares sit below it in that column. That tells you who will be on move when it opens \u2014 and therefore whether the threat is yours or theirs.")
B.mB=new A.h6(6,"onlyMove")
B.Ff=s([1,3,0,3,3,3,6,2,2,6,4,2,4,3,0],t.t)
B.DX=new A.ce(14,"The only move on the board",B.bw,"Late in a tight game, positions stop having good moves and bad ones. They have one move and six mistakes. This is such a position, checked move by move all the way to the end of the game.",B.mB,B.Ff,2,B.an,"Column 3 wins by force. Every other legal column throws the win away and loses. You are not expected to see 21 plies ahead \u2014 nobody does. You are expected to notice when a position has gone sharp, and to slow down when it has.")
B.Fe=s([2,4,0,3,5,3,3,3,2,2,1,5,2,4,3,1],t.t)
B.Eg=s([1,6],t.t)
B.DR=new A.ce(15,"Two poisoned columns",B.bw,"Almost every blunder is a move played without asking what it opens. Here two different columns lose on the spot, and neither of them looks dangerous.",B.iV,B.Fe,null,B.Eg,"Columns 2 and 7 each lift the teal diamonds into a four. Every other column is playable. Two questions before each move, in order: what line did their last piece extend, and what square would mine make reachable?")
B.FL=s([0,3,1,3,6,3],t.t)
B.DE=new A.ce(16,"Stopping a stack is cheap",B.bw,"A vertical threat only ever needs one square, and that square is always the very next drop in the column. It is the one threat you can always answer directly.",B.fj,B.FL,3,B.an,"Three teal diamond pieces in column 4. Dropping there takes the fourth square and the threat is simply gone \u2014 no counting, no side effects. Compare that with a horizontal threat, which has two ends.")
B.FE=s([3,2,3,1,2,5,0,2,3,0,4,2,2,3,3,2],t.t)
B.DH=new A.ce(17,"Make them answer you",B.bw,"A move that forces a reply keeps the initiative. If you spend the game responding to them, you only need to be wrong once. Here one move takes the game away from them entirely.",B.fk,B.FE,4,B.an,"Column 5 leaves two squares that each finish a four for the amber sunbursts. Initiative is not aggression; it is being the side that decides what the position is about. A fork is simply the strongest form of it.")
B.Fi=s([3,2,4,3,3,5,3,3,0,6,1,5,3],t.t)
B.DQ=new A.ce(18,"A fork from the other side",B.bw,"Forks are not only yours to make. The same shape, seen from the other chair, is the thing you have to prevent \u2014 and the way to learn to see it is to make it a few times yourself.",B.fk,B.Fi,4,B.an,"Column 5 gives the teal diamonds two squares that each complete a four. Next time you are on the receiving end of this shape, you will recognise it a move earlier \u2014 which is exactly when it can still be stopped.")
B.Gd=s([6,0,5,1,4,2],t.t)
B.DI=new A.ce(19,"When you both have threats",B.bw,"Two live threats do not cancel out. The one that can be played sooner wins, and whose move it is decides that.",B.fi,B.Gd,3,B.an,"Both sides have three on the bottom row here. It looks balanced and it is not: it is your move, so the position is simply won. When you both threaten, stop evaluating and start counting turns.")
B.FI=s([5,4,1,5,1,0,0,4,1,0,2,1,4,1,4,6,4,4],t.t)
B.DS=new A.ce(20,"Play the move you can explain",B.bw,"The habit worth keeping is small: before you drop a piece, say in one sentence what it does. Wins, blocks, builds, or takes a square they want. Here exactly one column can be explained, and it is the only one that wins.",B.mB,B.FI,2,B.an,"Column 3 forces a win five plies from here; every other column loses. This is the whole course in one position. Every lesson before it was a category of sentence you might say about a move \u2014 and after each match, the insight screen shows the one move where the game turned, so you can read it against the sentence you told yourself at the time.")
